      "type": "paragraph",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Little tour of vintage atelier above Sag Harbor store",
          "bold": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Rebecca Hessel Cohen built LoveShackFancy simply from a wedding dress she designed for herself in 2013, and somehow that single rose-colored ruffled piece grew into a company with two dozen boutiques and a $360M net worth- this kind of backstory makes “girl” read less like a visual identity and more like a business strategy. What the brand sells isn't really distinct from how Rebecca lives; her house, her closet, even her daughter’s bat mitzvah all get staged within the same “pink, romantic, girly” world her clothes are designed around, to the point where it's hard to tell if she's showing her life or restaging her storefront. That’s part of why her audience splits so unevenly… the fashion and weddings crowd she's technically selling to is only one piece of it, while a much bigger and messier group watches specifically to decide whether any of this is aspirational or just excessive. "
        }
      ]
